	<key>recovery.section.1</key>
	<string>While a session is active, Wakecup temporarily asks macOS to stay awake. Select “Keep on” under “Display backlight” to prevent the screen from turning off. These temporary requests are released when the session stops or the app exits.

Keep awake with lid closed additionally enables a system-wide sleep block: SleepDisabled becomes 1. When the session ends, a separate guardian process returns it to 0 and checks the result. It also attempts to restore sleep if the main app crashes. If restoration fails, use the manual steps above.

Wakecup does not change sleep timers, display timers, or hibernation settings.

If you installed the optional helper, Wakecup also adds a system service. It does not change sleep settings while idle. You can remove it in Wakecup Settings → Closed-lid permission → Remove helper….</string>
	<key>recovery.section.2</key>
	<string>1. Open Wakecup from the cup icon in the menu bar. If the icon is missing, press Command (⌘) + Space, type Wakecup, then press Return.
2. If you see Stop, click it and wait for the session to end. If you see Start, the session is already stopped.
3. If Restore Normal Sleep appears, click it. If macOS asks for permission, enter an administrator password.
4. Test sleep: choose Apple menu  → Sleep. If Sleep is unavailable or your Mac still will not sleep, continue with the manual steps below.</string>
	<key>recovery.section.3</key>
	<string>To open Terminal or Activity Monitor, press Command (⌘) + Space, type the app name, then press Return.

1. If Wakecup is running, open the gear menu and choose Quit Wakecup. If it is unresponsive, find Wakecup in Activity Monitor and stop the process using the stop button.
2. Quit any other apps that keep your Mac awake. In Terminal, run /usr/bin/pmset -g and check SleepDisabled. If it is 0 or absent, skip the reset and see “If the block is off but your Mac still will not sleep” below. If it remains 1 and you want to clear the system-wide sleep block, run the command below.
3. Enter your Mac administrator password and press Return. Terminal does not display password characters, not even asterisks; this is normal.

This command removes the system-wide sleep block, including one enabled by another app. No output after the command is normal; check the result in the next step.</string>
	<key>recovery.section.4</key>
	<string>Paste the command below into Terminal and press Return. Near the start of the output, look for SleepDisabled:
• 0, or no such line — the system-wide sleep block is off.
• 1 — the sleep block is still on.

Once the block is off, test Apple menu  → Sleep again.

If the recovery command completed without errors but the value stays at 1 or changes back, save your work and restart your Mac. After restarting, leave the Wakecup session off and repeat the manual recovery steps. If the command reports a password or permissions error, ask your Mac administrator for help.</string>
	<key>recovery.section.5</key>
	<string>If your Mac sleeps from the Apple menu but not automatically when idle, check your normal macOS sleep settings. Video playback, downloads, and other apps can also prevent sleep; stop them and test again.

The command below does not change anything. It lists apps currently requesting that your Mac stay awake. If the cause is unclear, save its output when asking for help.</string>
